# Line endings are a property of THIS REPOSITORY, not of whoever clones it.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS, and why it landed before the desktop was set up rather than after.
# Until now the working tree's byte content was a function of ONE machine's `core.autocrlf`
# — a per-user Git setting this repo has no say over, defaulted to `true` by Git for Windows.
# With a single machine that is invisible. Add a second and it becomes a variable, and every
# content-inspecting control here sits downstream of it: `@enforced` marker parsing, the probe
# harness's plant needles, the CLAUDE.md checker, spine comparison. A defect class that appears
# on some machines and not others is the worst debugging shape there is, and cloning the repo
# onto a second machine is exactly the condition that produces it.
#
# It is not hypothetical. On 2026-08-20 a routine branch checkout converted this tree to CRLF
# and two probes in `scripts/probe-checks.mjs` reported PLANT FAILED — LF needles finding
# nothing — with no line of their subject matter changed. `npm run check` is the LAST step of
# SETUP-NEW-PC.ps1, so a fresh clone would have ended its bootstrap on a red gate pointing at
# the wrong file entirely.
#
# The cost of pinning is a one-time, visible, reviewable working-tree rewrite. The cost of not
# pinning recurs, and arrives disguised as something else.
#
# NOTE ON THE DIFF: committing this stages no content churn. `core.autocrlf` was already
# converting CRLF to LF on the way IN, so every blob in history is LF today. What changes is
# what a checkout WRITES OUT — which is the half that was varying per machine.

# Text is stored LF in the repo and checked out LF everywhere. `auto` still lets Git's own
# heuristic exclude anything that is actually binary, so this is a floor, not an override.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts are pinned LF regardless of platform: a CRLF line ending becomes part of the
# shebang argument, and the interpreter fails with a message naming a path that looks correct.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The one direction that must NOT be LF. cmd.exe requires CRLF and fails obscurely without it.
# No such file is tracked today; the rule is here so that adding one cannot silently break it,
# which is the same reason the binary list below is exhaustive rather than as-needed.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary assets — never normalised, never diffed as text. Enumerated from what this repo
# actually tracks (jpg, png, docx, pdf, woff, ico) plus the near neighbours of each, because
# the failure mode of a MISSING entry is a corrupted asset, and it is silent until something
# tries to open the file.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.webp  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.eot   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.docx  binary
*.xlsx  binary
*.zip   binary
